  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe weather stays consistent during the latter half of the week, \nwith slight moderation of overnight temperatures as moisture levels \nstabilize. Afternoon highs continue to stay in the mid 80s to low \n90s with overnight lows in the 60s through the weekend.\n\nAn upper-level trough is forecast to amplify as it moves across the \narea Thursday through Saturday. The trajectory of the trough is not \nespecially favorable to rain chances with its track keeping our \nregion under more confluent flow aloft. Areas along the Rio Grande \nhave about a 20% chance of showers late Thursday to early Friday as \nthe passing trough interacts with some westward advection of low-\nlevel moisture. Otherwise, models have trended more towards the base \nof the trough sliding south of our area, with the rest of South-\nCentral Texas remaining under more stable conditions. Depending on \nthe track of the tropical disturbance in the western Gulf, there may \nbe a few isolated coastal showers in the Coastal Plains Friday into \nthe weekend. Models have come into more of an agreement in keeping \nthat system well to our east, so the impacts to our area will be \nminor to negligible. \n\nOnce the passing trough clears the area, ridging will rebuild over \nTexas ahead of a deep trough over the Pacific coast. After a long \nand somewhat uncommon streak of north winds, this will cause the low-\nlevel flow to revert back to a more conventional southerly wind, \nleading an increase in dew points and moisture early next week. This \nsupports broader rain chances, with the medium-range guidance \nsuggesting likelihood increasing towards mid-October.",
